var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"referencia/modelo/#Templates-and-environments","page":"Templates and environments","text":"The path of a document: an Environment says which layers exist; load_template reads and analyses; check confronts the data; render writes.","title":"Templates and environments"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/modelo/#Registering-names","page":"Templates and environments","text":"What a layer registers in the environment is a name, and names are local. Behaviour is methods, and methods are global and additive; the split is in D-025.","title":"Registering names"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/modelo/#Lookup","page":"Templates and environments","text":"","title":"Lookup"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.Environment","page":"Templates and environments","text":"Environment(; locale = nothing, domains = [])\n\nThe frozen vocabulary of one configuration of layers. Immutable: there is no register_*! on an Environment.\n\nBuilt in a deterministic, visible order: core, language layer, then each domain in the order of the vector. A name conflict is an error here, with both domains in the message.\n\nEnvironment() with no arguments is the bare core, the environment of the neutrality test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Environment"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.EnvironmentBuilder","page":"Templates and environments","text":"EnvironmentBuilder\n\nConstruction state of an Environment. It is mutable; the Environment is not. Only the builder has register_*!, and it exists only while the environment is being built, so an environment already in use cannot be mutated.\n\ndomain is the layer currently being applied. The constructor keeps it up to date so that a conflict message can name both culprits without the layer having to identify itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.EnvironmentBuilder"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.load_template","page":"Templates and environments","text":"load_template(env, path; root = dirname(path)) -> Model\n\nLike load_string, reading from disk. root is the directory fragments may be included from; it defaults to the template's own directory, and nothing outside it can be loaded (D-005).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.load_template"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.load_string","page":"Templates and environments","text":"load_string(env, text; name = \"<string>\") -> Model\n\nParses and analyses a template and returns it ready to use. Throws KanonSyntaxError if the text is malformed and KanonReferenceError if a path, type or formatter does not resolve.\n\nA syntax error skips analysis: there is no tree to validate (§10.3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.load_string"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.Model","page":"Templates and environments","text":"Model\n\nA loaded template: the syntax tree, its analysis, and the environment it was analysed in.\n\nTemplate is the bare syntax tree and knows no environment; Model is the template ready to use. Keeping them apart is what allows the same tree to be analysed in two environments, which the neutrality test does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Model"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_type!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_type!(b, T::Type; aliases = (,))\n\nGives the Julia type T a name in the language. The canonical name comes from kanon_typename(T); aliases gives per-language names (aliases = (pt = :dinheiro,)), and only the one for the active language enters the environment.\n\nWhat the type does (format, validate, decode, compare) does not go through here: it is methods of the generic functions in types.jl, defined in the layer's module (D-019).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_type!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_type_alias!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_type_alias!(b, alias, canonical)\n\nGives an already registered type a name in the active language. This is how dinheiro becomes an alias of money: the core registers its own types and, being neutral, has no aliases to give, and register_type! only accepts aliases from whoever registers the type (D-025).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_type_alias!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_attribute_alias!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_attribute_alias!(b, lang, alias, canonical)\n\nGives the attribute canonical a name in language lang: quando pontos é preciso in a pt template asks the same as when pontos is precise. Applied only when lang is the active language.\n\nIt is additive: the canonical name keeps working, and no template changes meaning. An attribute is not a keyword, so D-003 does not apply, just as a type name coexists with its alias.\n\nThe language layer registers it, not the layer that defines the attribute: preciso is a Portuguese word, and KanonScience has no language (the glossary form of D-056). A canonical name may have more than one alias, and does when the adjective agrees in gender with the field (a estimativa é precisa, o ponto é preciso). The first one registered is the one the engine writes back in messages.\n\nThe alias cannot be a keyword of the language: verdadeiro is already true, and the parser would read it as a keyword before the environment is consulted (D-076).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_attribute_alias!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_formatter_alias!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_formatter_alias!(b, lang, alias, canonical)\n\nGives the formatter canonical a name in language lang: {nome:maiusculo} in a pt template is {nome:upper}. Applied only when lang is the active language, additive, and registered by the language layer: the same rules as register_attribute_alias!.\n\nNot to be confused with a formatter of a language, such as extenso: that is behaviour, exists through dispatch and is declared by kanon_format_locale (D-026). An alias is only a name, and formats nothing the canonical formatter does not (D-076).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_formatter_alias!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_aliases!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_aliases!(b, lang, forms)\n\nThe written forms of the keywords in language lang. Accepts a NamedTuple ((data = \"dados\", when = \"quando\")) or any iterable of pairs ([:data => \"dados\", Symbol(\"for\") => \"para\"]); the second form exists because for, and and other keywords of the language are reserved in Julia and cannot be NamedTuple keys.\n\nApplied only when lang is the active language.\n\nThe corresponding English form stops being a keyword: a file that declares pt writes dados, not data. Mixing them is an error (D-003).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_aliases!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_block_style!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_block_style!(b, name; unit, layout, separator, number, ref)\n\nA domain's block style. unit is the marker unit; two domains registering the same unit is an error here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_block_style!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_inflection!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_inflection!(b, lang; marks, apply)\n\nThe language's inflection marks and the function that applies them. apply receives (word, mark, subject, ctx) and returns the whole word already inflected, which is what allows portador(a) → portadoras without the core knowing how to pluralise (D-013).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_inflection!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_repair_hook!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_repair_hook!(b, lang, hook)\n\nSeam repair hook, called after elision with (text, seams, ctx). Repair is local to the seam, never global (D-014).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_repair_hook!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_list_joiner!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_list_joiner!(b, lang, f)\n\nHow the language joins the elements of a list. f receives the already formatted parts and returns the whole text: [\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"] ⟶ \"a, b e c\".\n\nThe core joins with \", \", its only typographic convention, stated as such in §3.3 and explicitly replaceable. The replacement is a hook in the environment, not a method on AbstractVector: a method would be global and additive, so loading the layer would change the output of the bare core and break the neutrality that F6 checks (D-025).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_list_joiner!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_currency!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_currency!(b, code, symbol)\n\nA currency's symbol. money:symbol uses what is declared here; the core embeds no national convention (§3.3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_currency!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_separators!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"register_separators!(b; decimal, group)\n\nThe language's decimal and thousands separators. The core prints 0.42 and 1200; the language layer is what makes them 0,42 and 1.200 (§3.3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_separators!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.register_date_pattern!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"Date pattern for date:numeric. The factory default is ISO.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.register_date_pattern!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.configure!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"configure!(b::EnvironmentBuilder)\n\nThe core's registration: its types and factory defaults. Defined in core_types.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.configure!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.configure_locale!","page":"Templates and environments","text":"configure_locale!(b, ::Val{lang})\n\nExtension point for a language layer. Extenso.jl defines Kanon.configure_locale!(b, ::Val{:pt}); loading the module is enough for the language to exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.configure_locale!"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.typefor","page":"Templates and environments","text":"The Julia type registered under name, resolving language aliases. nothing if there is none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.typefor"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.typenames","page":"Templates and environments","text":"Every type name that can be declared in this environment, sorted, for error messages and editor completion. Collection behaviour and its aliases are left out: it exists, but is written through cardinality (D-071).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.typenames"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.stylefor","page":"Templates and environments","text":"Block style for the marker unit, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.stylefor"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.hasmark","page":"Templates and environments","text":"Is the inflection mark registered? If not, analyze treats the candidate as prose.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.hasmark"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.currency_symbol","page":"Templates and environments","text":"Currency symbol, or the code itself when the environment declares no symbol (§3.3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.currency_symbol"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/modelo/#Kanon.FormatContext","page":"Templates and environments","text":"FormatContext\n\nWhat a formatter may consult. Deliberately small: the environment and today.\n\ntoday is injected, never read from the clock, which makes determinism something that can be checked (§2.2). A template that uses today, rendered without it, is a contract error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.FormatContext"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/tipos/#Types-and-extension","page":"Types and extension","text":"A Kanon type is a set of methods of the core's generic functions. There is no hand-kept dispatch table, no mutable registry, and no eval.","title":"Types and extension"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/tipos/#The-declarative-form","page":"Types and extension","text":"","title":"The declarative form"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/tipos/#Core-types","page":"Types and extension","text":"","title":"Core types"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/tipos/#Protocol-failures","page":"Types and extension","text":"These are exceptions, not diagnostics: they happen outside a template file, so they have no line or column. check and analyze turn them into diagnostics where there is a position to attribute them to.","title":"Protocol failures"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_typename","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_typename(::Type{T}) -> Symbol\n\nThe type's name in the language. Required: it is what makes a Julia type a Kanon type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_typename"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_schema","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_schema(::Type{T}) -> Tuple{Vararg{FieldSpec}}\n\nThe fields of a composite type, in declaration order. () for a scalar, the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_schema"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_validate","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_validate(v, ctx) -> Nothing | Vector{Diagnostic}\n\nChecks on the value beyond its Julia type. The default checks nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_validate"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.format","page":"Types and extension","text":"format(v, ::Val{name}, ctx) -> AbstractString\n\nA formatter. Val{:default} is required for every type; the others are optional and listed by kanon_formats.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe default is the symbol, which falls back to the currency code when the environment declares none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.format"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_formats","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_formats(::Type{T}) -> Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}\n\nThe named formatters of T, sorted, without :default, which cannot be written in an interpolation: the default is just {price}.\n\nThe default implementation inspects the method table of format for signatures Tuple{typeof(format), T′, Val{S}, Any} with T <: T′ and S::Symbol. This is what keeps the promise of docs/api-extensao.md §2.1: a formatter added by plain dispatch, through no facade, exists for template validation and for error messages with no extra registration.\n\nThis inspects the method table, not user data: it runs no template code and is deterministic because the result is sorted.\n\nA layer may override this method to document its formatters, or to cover formatters defined generically (format(v::T, ::Val{N}, ctx) where {N}), which inspection cannot enumerate.\n\n\n\n\n\nkanon_formats(T, env) -> Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}\n\nThe formatters of T visible in this environment: the language-independent ones plus those of the active language. This is the list validation uses and error messages show.\n\nkanon_formats(T) on its own still lists everything that exists in the process; that is the protocol, and it knows no environment. The filtering happens here (D-026).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_formats"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_format_locale","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_format_locale(::Type{T}, ::Val{name}) -> Symbol | Nothing\n\nThe language a formatter belongs to, or nothing if it applies in any environment. The default is nothing; a language layer declares its own.\n\nIt exists because Julia methods are global and additive: without this declaration, loading Extenso in the process would make {preco:extenso} valid in a neutral environment, and an English report would come out with mil e duzentos reais. Behaviour is still dispatch; what the environment decides is visibility (D-026).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_format_locale"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_attribute","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_attribute(v, ::Val{name}) -> Bool\n\nA named predicate, used in the rules plane (when property is rural).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_attribute"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_attributes","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_attributes(::Type{T}) -> Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}\n\nThe attributes of T, sorted, to validate rules without data and for error messages.\n\npresent and absent are not listed here: they apply to every field and belong to the engine, not to the type (see UNIVERSAL_ATTRIBUTES).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_attributes"},{"category":"constant","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.UNIVERSAL_ATTRIBUTES","page":"Types and extension","text":"UNIVERSAL_ATTRIBUTES\n\nAttributes every field has, whatever its type. They belong to the engine: they follow from whether the value is present, and no type implements them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.UNIVERSAL_ATTRIBUTES"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_getfield","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_getfield(v, ::Val{name})\n\nReads a field declared by kanon_schema. The default is getproperty(v, name), which works when the field's name in the schema is the Julia property name; a type whose schema does not mirror its struct defines its own methods.\n\nIt exists because the schema is the type's interface and the struct is its implementation: without separating them, kanon_schema would become a promise about Julia field names, and renaming an internal field would break existing templates (D-023).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_getfield"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_decode","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_decode(::Type{T}, raw, ctx) -> T\n\nConverts a value from external input (JSON, a spreadsheet, a DataFrame) to the type. It is the only conversion point: there is no implicit coercion anywhere (§3.4).\n\nThe default accepts what is already of the type and rejects everything else, failing loudly with the type's name in the message.\n\n\n\n\n\nMoney is not decoded from a bare number: the currency would be missing, and guessing it would be the implicit coercion that §3.4 forbids. Accepts a Money, or an amount and a currency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_decode"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_json_schema","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_json_schema(::Type{T}) -> Nothing | JSON shape\n\nThe JSON shape kanon_decode accepts for T, as JSON Schema: what the contract (contract) publishes under the type's $defs. The default is nothing, and the contract then derives the shape from kanon_schema.\n\nThe two descriptions are different things, which is why this function exists (D-079). kanon_schema is the read interface: the fields a template reaches with {pessoa.nome}. Input may require more than that (pessoa needs genero, which decides portador(a) and which no template writes), and a contract derived only from the schema forbade (additionalProperties: false) the key the decoder requires. The JSON data of five real templates, which the engine renders byte for byte, was rejected by their own contract in a JSON Schema validator.\n\nThe shape is written with vectors of pairs, which keep their order, so the contract can be compared with diff (D-009):\n\nKanon.kanon_json_schema(::Type{Pessoa}) = [\n    \"type\" => \"object\",\n    \"properties\" => [\n        \"nome\"   => [\"type\" => \"string\", \"title\" => \"Nome completo\"],\n        \"genero\" => [\"type\" => \"string\", \"enum\" => [\"f\", \"m\"], \"title\" => \"Gênero\"],\n    ],\n    \"required\" => [\"nome\", \"genero\"],\n    \"additionalProperties\" => false,\n]\n\nA reference to another type (\"$ref\" => \"#/$defs/pessoa\") is included in the contract along with it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_json_schema"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.kanon_compare","page":"Types and extension","text":"kanon_compare(a::T, b) -> Int\n\nOrdering for the rules plane (price > 0). Returns -1, 0 or 1. The default refuses: comparison requires the type to declare how.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.kanon_compare"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.FieldSpec","page":"Types and extension","text":"FieldSpec\n\nA field of a composite type. optional is what extends the gap theorem into composites: if person.spouse is optional, {seller.spouse.name} is nullable and needs a group (docs/especificacao.md §3.1 and §14).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.FieldSpec"},{"category":"macro","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.@kanon_type","page":"Types and extension","text":"@kanon_type <name> <JuliaType> begin ... end\n\nDefines a Kanon type through dispatch, from a declarative description.\n\n@kanon_type person Pessoa begin\n    schema     = (FieldSpec(:name, :text), FieldSpec(:spouse, :person; optional = true))\n    getfield   = (name = :nome, spouse = :conjuge)\n    default    = (v, ctx) -> v.nome\n    formats    = (formal = (v, ctx) -> uppercase(v.nome),)\n    attributes = (minor = v -> v.idade < 18,)\n    validate   = (v, ctx) -> nothing\n    decode     = (raw, ctx) -> Pessoa(raw)\n    compare    = (a, b) -> cmp(a.nome, b)\n    locale     = :pt\nend\n\nOnly default is required. getfield only needs to list the fields whose name in the schema differs from the Julia property name.\n\naliases do not go here. A name is local to the environment and is registered in the layer's configure!, with register_type!(b, Pessoa; aliases = (pt = :pessoa,)): the split set by D-019 and confirmed by D-025.\n\nlocale, when present, declares that this type's named formatters belong to a language and are visible only where it is active (D-026).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.@kanon_type"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.Money","page":"Types and extension","text":"Money(amount, currency)\n\nA monetary value: an exact amount and a currency code. The amount is a Rational{Int128}, not a float, because a document that says R$ 1.234,57 cannot depend on binary rounding.\n\nThe core prints two decimal places. That is a stated convention, not a property of the currency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Money"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.NumberValue","page":"Types and extension","text":"NumberValue\n\nWhat counts as a number. Bool is left out on purpose: in Julia it is an Integer, but in Kanon boolean is a separate type; otherwise {flag:fixed2} would be valid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.NumberValue"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.KanonProtocolError","page":"Types and extension","text":"KanonProtocolError\n\nA failure of the type protocol: the type cannot do what was asked of it.\n\nThese are exceptions rather than diagnostics because they happen outside a template file, so they have no line or column. analyze and check catch them and turn them into diagnostics when there is a position to attribute them to.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.KanonProtocolError"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.UnregisteredType","page":"Types and extension","text":"The Julia type does not implement kanon_typename, so it is not a Kanon type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.UnregisteredType"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.UnknownFormatter","page":"Types and extension","text":"The type has no such formatter. The message lists the ones it has.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.UnknownFormatter"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.UnknownAttribute","page":"Types and extension","text":"The type has no such attribute. The message lists the ones it has.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.UnknownAttribute"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.UndecodableValue","page":"Types and extension","text":"A value from external input that the type cannot decode (§3.4: there is no implicit coercion).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.UndecodableValue"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/tipos/#Kanon.IncomparableValues","page":"Types and extension","text":"The type does not declare how to compare itself with that value (§8.1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.IncomparableValues"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/render/#Rendering","page":"Rendering","text":"render is pure: no I/O, no clock, no randomness. today is injected. If analyze and check passed, the only possible failure is the resource budget.","title":"Rendering"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/render/#Output-formats","page":"Rendering","text":"An interpolated value never changes the structure of the document (D-028): the author's prose passes through untouched, and the data is escaped.","title":"Output formats"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/render/#Elision-and-seam-repair","page":"Rendering","text":"","title":"Elision and seam repair"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.render","page":"Rendering","text":"render(bound; budget = Budget()) -> String\n\nRenders data that has already been validated. Pure: no I/O, no clock, no randomness.\n\n\n\n\n\nrender(model, data; today = nothing, budget = Budget(), to = PlainText()) -> String\n\nValidates and renders. Throws KanonContractError if the data does not satisfy the contract: there is no rendering without validation, not even as an option (§14).\n\nto selects the output format, as an OutputFormat or by name (:text, :markdown, :typst).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.render"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.preview","page":"Rendering","text":"preview(model, data; today = nothing) -> String\n\nThe draft: the document with «markers» where data has not arrived yet. It is not a lenient mode. It is a separate command, its output is always visibly marked, and render still refuses exactly the same data.\n\nWhat people usually want from a \"lenient mode\" is to see the text while the data is still being gathered. Serving that here is what lets the engine never relax anywhere else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.preview"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.Budget","page":"Rendering","text":"Budget\n\nLimits that are counted, never wall-clock time (D-010): a time limit would make whether the engine fails non-deterministic. The same input would fail on a loaded machine and pass on an idle one, and the golden suite would become flaky precisely in CI, where slow machines are.\n\nIt lives in this file because the first count happens here: the repetitions the rules produce are counted before any text is written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Budget"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.OutputFormat","page":"Rendering","text":"OutputFormat\n\nHow the text becomes a file. PlainText is the default and touches nothing; the others escape what their format would read as markup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.OutputFormat"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.PlainText","page":"Rendering","text":"Plain text: nothing is markup, nothing needs escaping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.PlainText"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.Markdown","page":"Rendering","text":"Markdown. Escapes what CommonMark would read as markup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Markdown"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.Typst","page":"Rendering","text":"Typst. A candidate page backend: Kanon guarantees the content and Typst lays out the page (estado-da-arte.md).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Typst"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.output_format","page":"Rendering","text":"output_format(name) -> OutputFormat\n\nThe output format by name. Fails naming the formats that exist: an unknown format would silently write the wrong document, the last thing this engine should do.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.output_format"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.escape_value","page":"Rendering","text":"escape_value(fmt, text, at_line_start) -> String\n\nEscapes a value that comes from the data. It does not apply to the template's prose: an author who writes **important** in a Markdown template wants bold, and removing it would be editing what they typed, the same mistake a global seam repair would be (D-014).\n\nat_line_start says whether the value is being emitted at the start of a line, which only the renderer knows. Without it, a 12.345 in the middle of a sentence would have to be escaped as if it could open a list, and no value would come out readable.\n\n\n\n\n\nEscapes a value for Markdown.\n\nTracks line starts because a value can contain line breaks: `\"X\n\nCláusula\"` would\n\nput a heading in the document if the # were not escaped there, and escaping it everywhere would fill the text with backslashes without protecting anything.\n\n\n\n\n\nEscapes a value for Typst.\n\nThe backslash goes before the digit, not before the dot: 1. x at the start of a line is an explicitly numbered list item, and the way to write it as a paragraph is \\1. x, the opposite of Markdown, where the dot takes the backslash. Two markup languages, two rules, which is why escaping is a method per format and not one function.\n\nThree constructs do not open anything visible; they delete, checked against Typst 0.15.1 while compiling the golden output of real template no. 12 (D-062):\n\n// starts a comment anywhere on a line: processo 123//2026, vara comes out as processo 123, and the rest of the line disappears without any error;\n~ is a non-breaking space: ~200 m² loses its tilde;\n-? is a soft hyphen, and disappears entirely.\n\nThese are the worst kind of defect a format can have: the document compiles and says less than the data did. The slash is escaped only where it forms a comment: next to another slash, or at the edge of the value, where the neighbour is prose the escaper cannot see.\n\nThree more of the same family were found by taking that check to its end, sweeping the whole alphabet against the compiler rather than following a suspicion (D-080). These do not delete, they replace, which is why D-062 missed them:\n\n-- is an en dash and --- an em dash: SEI 0001--2026 comes out as SEI 0001–2026;\n... is an ellipsis: disse que ... não sabia comes out with ….\n\nA case number that changes a character in a document going to be signed is the same failure as //, made worse by being readable: nobody checks what looks right. The hyphen is escaped by the slash rule, which is the rule for every two-character shortcut: next to another hyphen, or at the edge. The dot is not: an ellipsis needs three, and only a sequence inside the value itself can form one. A single dot at the edge would need the prose to supply the other two, and then the ellipsis is the author's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.escape_value"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.heading","page":"Rendering","text":"heading(fmt, level, text) -> String\n\nThe label of a block whose style declares layout = :heading. In plain text it is the paragraph the style produced; in a format with headings it becomes a real heading, with the level taken from the depth of the numbering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.heading"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.line_breaks","page":"Rendering","text":"line_breaks(fmt, paragraph) -> String\n\nLine breaks inside a paragraph, which §4.1 preserves and leaves to the format to make hard or not. In plain text the break is already the line the author wrote. In Markdown and Typst a single newline is a space, so a format that decided nothing was in effect joining lines: Nestes termos, and pede deferimento. came out on one line in the .docx, and the signature block became PAULO HENRIQUE LINS OAB/PE 12.345, with no warning, while the plain-text output of the same template was right (D-070, real template no. 15).\n\nA hard break is a backslash at the end of the line, which CommonMark, pandoc's default reader and Typst all read the same way, and which is visible in the file, unlike the two trailing spaces every editor strips. It is added only where the break would otherwise be a space: in runs of consecutive lines where none opens a block construct. A list, table or heading written by the author is their markup and passes through untouched (D-028); a backslash there would break the table and appear literally at the end of the item.\n\nThe question is asked of the already escaped text: a value that starts a line with # or 1. arrives here as \\# and 1\\. and opens nothing, which is what makes the rule safe against the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.line_breaks"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.repair","page":"Rendering","text":"repair(chars, seams) -> (text, seams)\n\nApplies the procedure of §5.2: merges adjacent seams, applies R1–R5 once from left to right, and removes lines left empty by a seam.\n\nseams are positions in the text as it came out of elision: seam p lies between chars[p-1] and chars[p]. It also returns the positions after the repair, because those are what the language hook needs; passing the earlier ones would point it at the wrong text (§5.4).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.repair"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.BlockInstance","page":"Rendering","text":"BlockInstance\n\nOne occurrence of a block in the output. subject is the subject's value (the current element, when the block repeats) and number is the number it took, empty if the block is not numbered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.BlockInstance"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/render/#Kanon.Plan","page":"Rendering","text":"Plan\n\nWhat the rules decided for this data: the instances in output order, and each block's number for cross-references.\n\nnumbers[pos] is empty for a removed or unnumbered block. A repeated block cannot be cross-referenced (K2034, checked without data), so a cross-reference number is never ambiguous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Plan"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Tools","page":"Tools","text":"","title":"Tools"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Ingestion","page":"Tools","text":"Package extensions, loaded when Tables.jl or JSON3.jl is present. The core depends on neither.","title":"Ingestion"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Including-fragments","page":"Tools","text":"","title":"Including fragments"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Inspection","page":"Tools","text":"What an editing tool consumes: the template's structure, already resolved, without reimplementing the analysis (D-029).","title":"Inspection"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Command-line","page":"Tools","text":"The CLI logic is in Kanon.main, which takes the streams as arguments and returns the exit code instead of ending the process, so the five exit codes can be tested without a child process.\n\nWhat that does not test is loading: inside a test suite that has already loaded the layer and JSON3, --domain and reading .json have nothing to load. The flow with a layer is tested through bin/kanon, in a separate process (D-059, D-060).\n\nkanon check    template.kanon [data.json]\nkanon render   template.kanon data.json [-o out.md] [--to markdown|typst]\nkanon contract template.kanon\nkanon preview  template.kanon [data.json]\nkanon outline  template.kanon\nkanon ask      template.kanon [data] [-o data.json]\n\nWith a domain layer: --locale pt --domain KanonLegal. For Portuguese with no domain layer, the language package is loaded the same way: --locale pt --domain Extenso (D-068). ask asks for every missing scalar field, checks each answer with check, and writes the data back in the shape it came in: JSON if the input was JSON or if -o ends in .json (D-061). A number written the way the document prints it is refused, showing the expected form, and an ambiguous one (1.320, in a language where the dot separates thousands) is refused with both readings (D-069).\n\nExit codes: 0 success, 1 contract, 2 template, 3 usage, 4 resources.","title":"Command line"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Kanon.rows","page":"Tools","text":"rows(table)\n\nThe rows of a table, as data. Requires Tables.jl to be loaded: the core does not depend on it, and the extension exists only when it does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.rows"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Kanon.render_each","page":"Tools","text":"render_each(model, table; today, budget) -> Vector{String}\n\nOne document per row of the table. Requires Tables.jl to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.render_each"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Kanon.read_json","page":"Tools","text":"read_json(path) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nReads data from a JSON file. Requires JSON3.jl to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.read_json"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Kanon.parse_json","page":"Tools","text":"parse_json(text) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nLike read_json, from a string. Requires JSON3.jl to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.parse_json"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Kanon.Loader","page":"Tools","text":"Loader\n\nWhere fragments may come from. root is the configured root, and nothing outside it can be loaded: not by absolute path, not by .., not by symbolic link.\n\nWithout a root, inclusion is refused: by default no file is read, and whoever wants to include says from where.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Loader"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Kanon.compose","page":"Tools","text":"compose(template, loader, keywords) -> Template\n\nResolves inclusions and returns the composed template: a single Template in which nothing shows there was a fragment, so analysis and rendering do not know about inclusion.\n\nNode identifiers stay unique because every file is parsed with the same counter, which avoids rebuilding the tree to renumber it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.compose"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Kanon.outline","page":"Tools","text":"outline(model) -> Vector{BlockOutline}\n\nThe template's skeleton, in file order, which is output order because rules only remove and repeat blocks (D-015).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.outline"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Kanon.BlockOutline","page":"Tools","text":"BlockOutline\n\nA block as a tool needs to see it: what it is, what governs it, and what it uses.\n\nrule and foreach are the text of the rule attached to the block (the editor's middle column), and number is the number it takes when nothing is removed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.BlockOutline"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Kanon.FieldUse","page":"Tools","text":"FieldUse\n\nA field used by a block: where, with which formatter, and whether it may be absent.\n\nguarded is what the editor's middle column shows in red when missing: a nullable field must sit inside a group, and the editor can say so while the author types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.FieldUse"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/ferramentas/#Kanon.format_outline","page":"Tools","text":"format_outline(io, model)\n\nThe skeleton as aligned text: the block on the left, the rule on the right.\n\nThese are the editor's first two columns, in the terminal, and they answer the question the roadmap calls risk 16.2: what comes out of this template, and under which condition?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.format_outline"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/#Visão-geral-(português)","page":"Visão geral (pt)","text":"Linguagem de modelos de documento e seu motor.\n\nO modelo declara seu contrato de dados; o que não satisfaz o contrato não renderiza. Campo obrigatório ausente interrompe a geração com um erro que nomeia o campo e aponta a linha — nunca produz um documento com lacuna silenciosa.\n\nkanon 1\n\ndata\n  seller : text  !\n  price  : money !\n  notes  : text\n\ntext\n\n: preamble\n{seller} vende pelo preço de {price}[, observado que {notes}].\n\nOs colchetes são o ponto. notes é opcional, então tudo que depende dele fica dentro de um grupo — e quando o valor falta, o grupo inteiro sai, vírgula e tudo. Interpolar um valor opcional fora de um grupo é erro de validação, não surpresa em produção.","title":"Visão geral (português)"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/#O-teorema-da-lacuna","page":"Visão geral (pt)","text":"Se check(tmpl) passa e check(tmpl, dados) passa, então render(tmpl, dados) não contém nenhum trecho originado de valor ausente, nem trecho cuja leitura dependa de um.\n\nÉ a única afirmação do projeto para a qual o levantamento não encontrou precedente, e tudo o mais serve a ela. Não é slogan: a implicação é verificada nó a nó, e um teste de propriedade a afirma sobre um corpus.","title":"O teorema da lacuna"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/#Os-documentos-normativos","page":"Visão geral (pt)","text":"Esta referência descreve a API. O que a linguagem é está em documentos separados, no repositório, e eles são normativos:\n\nDocumento O que contém\nespecificacao.md léxico, gramática dos três planos, tipos, elisão, erros, o teorema\ndecisoes.md as decisões de projeto, com alternativas, motivo e data\napi-extensao.md o protocolo de extensão — dez funções genéricas\nast.md a árvore, as tabelas laterais e as quatro invariantes\nexemplos.md dois documentos reais, com a saída exigida byte a byte\nroadmap.md o estado de cada fase, e o que cada uma descobriu","title":"Os documentos normativos"},{"category":"section","location":"pt/#As-camadas","page":"Visão geral (pt)","text":"O núcleo não conhece idioma nem domínio. Ele numera 1, 2, 3.1; emite 0.42 e 2026-03-12; junta listas com \", \". Tudo além disso é camada:\n\nPacote O que acrescenta\nExtenso português: flexão por marca, extenso, datas, separadores\nKanonLegal pessoa, imovel, parte; o marcador § e CLÁUSULA PRIMEIRA\nKanonScience measure; o marcador @ e Theorem 1\n\nCamadas estendem por despacho múltiplo, nunca por registro:\n\nformat(v, ::Val{name}, ctx) = throw(UnknownFormatter(typeof(v), name))   # núcleo\nformat(v::Money, ::Val{:extenso}, ctx) = dinheiro_extenso(v.amount, v.currency)","title":"As camadas"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/validacao/#Validation","page":"Validation","text":"Three steps, three categories of error. Each has what the previous one lacked: parse has no environment, analyze has no data, render has both.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"referencia/validacao/#Diagnostics","page":"Validation","text":"Errors are user interface here, not an implementation detail. Every diagnostic has a stable code: the test suite asserts on the code, so the wording of a message can improve without breaking a test. The messages themselves are in Portuguese.","title":"Diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.analyze","page":"Validation","text":"analyze(env, tmpl) -> Analysis\n\nResolves the template against the environment. Does not mutate the Template and does not throw: it returns the Analysis with the accumulated diagnostics. load_template is what throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.analyze"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.Analysis","page":"Validation","text":"Analysis\n\nThe result of analyze: side tables indexed by NodeId, plus the accumulated diagnostics.\n\nFilled in by phase: paths and formatter come from F2.2; attribute, which holds the canonical name of an is written with a language alias, from D-076; guarded from F2.3; block_index, block_rule and block_foreach from F2.4; numbering from F5.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Analysis"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.ResolvedPath","page":"Validation","text":"ResolvedPath\n\nWhat a path written in the template means, once the environment has been consulted.\n\nnullable is the field the gap theorem relies on: it is true if any segment of the path is optional. Optionality propagates through composite types; otherwise the theorem would hold only at the first level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.ResolvedPath"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.check","page":"Validation","text":"check(model, data; today = nothing) -> DiagnosticSet\n\nDoes the data satisfy the contract? An empty set means yes.\n\nDeliberately does not throw: check asks the question, and a \"no\" should come with the whole list of what is missing, not just the first problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.check"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.bind","page":"Validation","text":"bind(model, data; today = nothing) -> Bound\n\nValidates the data against the contract and decodes what passes. Does not throw: it returns the Bound with the accumulated diagnostics and leaves the decision to the caller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.bind"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.Bound","page":"Validation","text":"Bound\n\nValidated and decoded data, ready to render. values is indexed by the field's position in template.data.fields: file order, which is also the order of the contract and of the errors.\n\nThe renderer decodes nothing again: everything that reaches it has been through here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Bound"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.contract","page":"Validation","text":"contract(model) -> String\n\nThe template's data contract, as JSON Schema draft 2020-12 with the x-kanon extension (docs/api-extensao.md §7).\n\nDeterministic: two calls on the same model return the same string, byte for byte.\n\n\n\n\n\nWrites the contract to a file, meant to be versioned next to the template.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.contract"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.Diagnostic","page":"Validation","text":"Diagnostic\n\nA problem found in a template or in its data. code is stable across versions and is listed in CODE_TITLES.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.Diagnostic"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.DiagnosticSet","page":"Validation","text":"DiagnosticSet\n\nAn ordered collection of diagnostics. The order is deterministic (file, line, column, code) so the output can be compared with diff and is stable in tests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.DiagnosticSet"},{"category":"constant","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.CODE_TITLES","page":"Validation","text":"CODE_TITLES\n\nRegistry of every diagnostic code. The title is the short line shown in the problem's header; the full message comes from where the diagnostic is emitted.\n\nOnce published, a code never changes meaning (compatibility policy, docs/especificacao.md §13). New codes are additive.\n\nEvery code here is emitted somewhere in src/ or lib/, and a test checks that. A code that is never raised misleads: it suggests the engine distinguishes a case it does not, and the first person to build a tool on it finds out the condition never happens. K1213 (unknown marker) and K1304 (unknown operator) were removed for that reason: the first is impossible by construction (the parser does not consult the environment, ast.md §8, and a marker without a style is reported as K2030), and the second was covered by K1302.\n\nK10xx  file structure and pragma\nK11xx  data plane\nK12xx  text plane\nK13xx  rules plane\nK20xx  references: paths, types and formatters\nK30xx  contract: the data against the contract\nK40xx  resources: the render budget\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.CODE_TITLES"},{"category":"function","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.format_diagnostics","page":"Validation","text":"format_diagnostics(io, set)\n\nWrites the diagnostics in the format of docs/especificacao.md §10.4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.format_diagnostics"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.KanonError","page":"Validation","text":"KanonError\n\nThe root of the engine's exceptions. Each error category in §10.1 has its own, and all of them carry a DiagnosticSet, never a bare message.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.KanonError"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.KanonSyntaxError","page":"Validation","text":"Malformed template. Reported when reading, with no data and no environment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.KanonSyntaxError"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.KanonReferenceError","page":"Validation","text":"Field, formatter or block that does not exist. Detectable without data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.KanonReferenceError"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.KanonContractError","page":"Validation","text":"Valid template, data that does not satisfy it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.KanonContractError"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.KanonEnvironmentError","page":"Validation","text":"Misconfigured environment: two domains claim the same name, or a layer registered something invalid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.KanonEnvironmentError"},{"category":"type","location":"referencia/validacao/#Kanon.KanonResourceError","page":"Validation","text":"The resource budget was exceeded: the only way rendering can fail (§11, D-010).\n\nIt lives here with the other exceptions, not in the renderer, because check can also exceed the budget: rules are evaluated before any text is produced.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Kanon.KanonResourceError"},{"category":"section","location":"#Kanon","page":"Overview","text":"Kanon is a template language for documents in which each template declares the data it needs. A template whose data does not satisfy that declaration does not render: a missing required field stops generation with an error naming the field and the line.\n\nEvery example on this page runs when the documentation is built, and uses only the core package — no language or domain layer.","title":"Kanon"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-first-template","page":"Overview","text":"using Kanon\n\nreport = load_string(Environment(), \"\"\"\nkanon 1\n\ndata\n  seller : text  !\n  price  : money !\n  notes  : text\n\ntext\n\n: preamble\n{seller} sells for {price}[, noting that {notes}].\n\"\"\"; name = \"report.kanon\")\n\nrender(report, Dict(\"seller\" => \"Ana\", \"price\" => Money(\"1200.50\", :USD),\n                    \"notes\" => \"payment in cash\"))\n\"Ana sells for USD 1200.50, noting that payment in cash.\"\n\n! marks a field as required. notes is optional, so the text that depends on it sits inside brackets, and when notes is absent the whole bracketed group is left out, comma included:\n\nrender(report, Dict(\"seller\" => \"Ana\", \"price\" => Money(\"1200.50\", :USD)))\n\"Ana sells for USD 1200.50.\"\n\nInterpolating an optional value outside a group is rejected when the template is loaded, before any data exists:\n\ntry\n    load_string(Environment(), \"\"\"\n    kanon 1\n\n    data\n      notes : text\n\n    text\n\n    : a\n    Note: {notes}.\n    \"\"\"; name = \"bad.kanon\")\ncatch err\n    showerror(stdout, err)\nend\nbad.kanon: 1 problema encontrado\n\n  referência, valor que pode faltar, fora de grupo opcional       [K2012]\n    linha 9, coluna 7: `notes` pode faltar, porque o contrato declara `notes` opcional, e está fora de qualquer grupo opcional.\n    Envolva o trecho que depende dele em colchetes: `[..., notes ...]`. Assim o trecho inteiro sai quando o valor falta, em vez de deixar um buraco.\n\nAnd data that leaves out a required field is rejected by check, which returns every problem at once instead of stopping at the first:\n\ncheck(report, Dict(\"seller\" => \"Ana\"))\nreport.kanon: 1 problema encontrado\n\n  contrato, campo obrigatório ausente                             [K3001]\n    linha 5, coluna 3: `price` é exigido pelo modelo (linha 5) e não foi informado.\n    Informe o valor, ou declare o campo opcional e envolva em `[...]` o trecho que depende dele.\n\n\nThe diagnostic messages are in Portuguese — see Language below. Each one carries a stable code (K2012, K3001), so tools and tests can match on the code rather than on the wording.","title":"A first template"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-guarantee","page":"Overview","text":"If check(tmpl) passes and check(tmpl, data) passes, then render(tmpl, data) contains no text originating from an absent value, and no text whose reading depends on one.\n\nMany template engines can fail on a missing field. What Kanon checks is narrower and, as far as the survey in docs/estado-da-arte.md found, not covered elsewhere: that no sentence of the output depends on a value that was not there. The check is done per node of the template, and a property test asserts it over a corpus.\n\nTwo rules follow from it:\n\nThere is no lenient mode. No option renders with an unsatisfied contract.\nNo type has a null of its own: there is no \"empty money\". Blank text is an error in a required field and is treated as absent, with a warning, in an optional one.\n\nSystem Where the contract lives Checked when Prevents gaps?\nLiquid, Mustache nowhere — no\nJinja2 StrictUndefined implicit at render only the field\nAskama (Rust) a Rust struct compile time only the field\nHotDocs a component file assembly only the field\ndocassemble YAML + Python interview asks for it\nKanon the template itself before render, without data yes","title":"The guarantee"},{"category":"section","location":"#Data,-text-and-rules","page":"Overview","text":"A template has three parts: data declares the contract, text is the document in the order it comes out, and rules decide which blocks appear.\n\nsale = load_string(Environment(), \"\"\"\nkanon 1\n\ndata\n  seller       : text   !\n  price        : money  !\n  installments : number\n\ntext\n\n: preamble\n{seller} sells for {price}.\n\n:: payment\nPaid in {installments} monthly instalments.\n\nrules\n  payment when installments > 1\n\"\"\"; name = \"sale.kanon\")\n\nprint(render(sale, Dict(\"seller\" => \"Ana\", \"price\" => Money(\"3600\", :USD),\n                        \"installments\" => 3)))\nAna sells for USD 3600.00.\n\n1. Paid in 3 monthly instalments.\n\nRules only remove or repeat blocks. They never insert, replace or reorder text, so reading the text part gives an upper bound on what the document can contain.","title":"Data, text and rules"},{"category":"section","location":"#Output-formats","page":"Overview","text":"render produces plain text by default, and also :markdown and :typst. The author's markup passes through; markup inside a value is escaped, so data cannot change the structure of the document:\n\nsigned = load_string(Environment(), \"\"\"\nkanon 1\n\ndata\n  name : text !\n\ntext\n\n: a\nSigned by **{name}**.\n\"\"\"; name = \"signed.kanon\")\n\nprint(render(signed, Dict(\"name\" => \"*Maria* # x\"); to = :markdown))\nSigned by **\\*Maria\\* # x**.\n\nFor .docx, .odt or PDF, pipe the Markdown through pandoc.","title":"Output formats"},{"category":"section","location":"#Command-line","page":"Overview","text":"kanon check    template.kanon [data.json]\nkanon render   template.kanon data.json -o out.md\nkanon contract template.kanon              # JSON Schema 2020-12\nkanon preview  template.kanon [data.json]  # marked draft, never exported\nkanon outline  template.kanon              # every block and the rule that governs it\nkanon ask      template.kanon [data.json]  # asks for each missing value\n\nThe exit codes are listed in the specification.","title":"Command line"},{"category":"section","location":"#Layers","page":"Overview","text":"The core package knows no natural language and no domain. It numbers blocks 1, 2, 3.1, prints 0.42 and 2026-03-12, and joins lists with \", \". Everything else is a layer in lib/:\n\nPackage Adds\nExtenso Portuguese: inflection, numbers and dates spelled out, separators\nKanonLegal pessoa, imovel, parte; the § marker and CLÁUSULA PRIMEIRA\nKanonScience measure (value with uncertainty); the @ marker and Theorem 1\n\nLayers extend the core by adding methods to its generic functions, not through a registry:\n\nformat(v, ::Val{name}, ctx) = throw(UnknownFormatter(typeof(v), name))   # core\nformat(v::Money, ::Val{:extenso}, ctx) = spell_out(v, ctx)               # Extenso\n\nThe test suite runs the core with no layer loaded and fails if anything language- or domain-specific is found in it.","title":"Layers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reuse-and-ingestion","page":"Overview","text":"include \"clauses.kanon\" pulls in a fragment, and the fragment's data declarations are merged with the host's. The loader only reads below a configured root.\n\nTables.jl and JSON3 are package extensions. With Tables loaded, render_each(tmpl, table) produces one document per row, and a dotted column name such as load.value becomes a field of a composite value.","title":"Reuse and ingestion"},{"category":"section","location":"#Language","page":"Overview","text":"The code, this page and the API reference are in English. The specification, the decision record and the diagnostic messages are in Portuguese, the author's language. Generated documents are not affected: the language a template is written in is chosen by the layers loaded, not by the engine.\n\nThe normative documents are in the repository: the specification, the decision record, the extension protocol and the roadmap.","title":"Language"},{"category":"section","location":"#Status","page":"Overview","text":"The syntax of language version 1 is frozen: the golden corpus in §13 of the specification must render byte-identical on every kanon 1.x engine. The Julia API is at 0.1 and may still change.","title":"Status"}]
}
